tel 1.0

base metamorphose
import soundness

info   permutations as reversible rearrangements, stored compactly as factorial-base Lehmer codes
detail
    _Metamorphose_ represents a permutation — a reversible rearrangement of a sequence — as a
    single number. A `Permutation` is built from the reordered indexes, applied to any list at
    least as long, and inverted; an index sequence which is not a permutation, with a duplicate
    or a gap, is a typed `Permutation.Error`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val shuffle = Permutation(Sequence(3, 1, 4, 2, 0, 5))
    val items = List(t"zero", t"one", t"two", t"three", t"four", t"five")

    shuffle(items)                             // List(t"three", t"one", t"four", t"two", t"zero", t"five")
    shuffle.inverse(shuffle(items)) == items   // true
    ```

    Internally a permutation is its [Lehmer code](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lehmer_code)
    written in the factorial number system, a `Factoradic`: one integer which uniquely
    identifies it, so permutations are compact to store and to enumerate, and
    `Permutation.bySize(n)` lists all `n!` of them in order. A permutation of *n* elements takes
    O(*n* log *n*) bits, the information-theoretic minimum. `Factorial` supplies the factorials
    the encoding is built on.

    The library has one source set, `metamorphose.core`, which compiles for every platform.
    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella.

refer metamorphose.Permutation
refer metamorphose.Factoradic
refer metamorphose.Factorial

entry .Permutation   a reversible rearrangement of a sequence, stored as its factoradic Lehmer code
  detail
      A `Permutation` is made from the reordered indexes of a sequence with `.apply`, which
      checks that they are exactly `0` to `n - 1` once each, or directly from its number with
      `Permutation(Factoradic(n))`; since the number *is* the permutation, the two agree:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Permutation(Factoradic(45)) == Permutation(Sequence(1, 4, 2, 3, 0))   // true

      val shuffle = Permutation(Sequence(3, 1, 4, 2, 0, 5))
      shuffle.lehmer      // the Lehmer code, as a List[Int]
      shuffle.expansion   // List(3, 1, 4, 2, 0, 5)
      ```

      Applied to a list, it rearranges the elements (`#apply`); `#inverse` undoes it. A
      permutation does not record the length it was built from, only the shortest suffix it
      moves: the identity of any size is `Permutation(Factoradic(0))`, and a permutation which
      leaves its leading elements in place has a shorter `#lehmer` than its index sequence.
      Applied to a longer list, it leaves the excess leading elements where they are and
      rearranges the rest.

      A permutation encodes as `Data` (`.encodable`), the big-endian two's-complement bytes of
      its factoradic number: `permutation.in[Data]`.
  refer metamorphose.Factoradic
  refer metamorphose.Permutation.Error
  entry .apply   a permutation from its reordered indexes, checked to be each of `0` to `n - 1` once
      `Permutation(Sequence(3, 1, 0, 2))` is the rearrangement which puts the element at index 3
      first, then index 1, and so on. It needs a `Tactic[Permutation.Error]`, raised with
      `InvalidIndex` for an index below 0 or not below the length, and `DuplicateIndex` for an
      index which appears twice.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Permutation(Sequence(5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0))(List(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6))   // List(6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1)
      safely(Permutation(Sequence(3, 1, 4, 2, 3, 5)))                     // Unset: 3 appears twice
      ```

      The case-class constructor `Permutation(factoradic)` makes one from its number, with no
      check, since every non-negative factoradic is a permutation.
  entry .bySize   every permutation of `n` elements, in order of their factoradic numbers
      A `Chain` of all `n!` permutations, from the identity (`Factoradic(0)`) to the reversal
      (`Factoradic(n! - 1)`). It is computed eagerly, so it is practical only for small `n`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val list = List(t"a", t"b", t"c")
      Permutation.bySize(3).map(_(list))   // all six orderings of a, b, c
      ```
  entry .encodable   encodes a permutation as `Data`: the bytes of its factoradic number
      The big-endian two's-complement bytes of `#factoradic`'s number, as `BigInt#toByteArray`
      gives them. There is no decoding given; read the bytes as a `BigInt` and wrap it in a
      `Factoradic` to recover the permutation.
  entry .Error   raised when indexes or a sequence do not make, or fit, a permutation
    detail
        Error 427: _could not construct permutation because `reason`_, where the reason is one
        of the `Permutation.Error.Reason` cases:

        ```tabular
        column  Case
        column  Code
        column  Message
        column  Note
        row  `BaseRange(value, base)`  1  _the value `value` is too large for its positional base `base`_  a digit given to `Factoradic(List(…))` is not below its position's base
        row  `DuplicateIndex(index, element)`  2  _the index `index` was duplicated at `element`_  `Permutation(Sequence(…))` met an index for the second time; `element` is the position at which it reappeared
        row  `InvalidIndex(last, max)`  3  _the index `last` appears, but every index should be in the range 0-`max`_  an index given to `Permutation(Sequence(…))` is negative or not below the sequence's length
        row  `TooShort(length, min)`  4  _the input, of size `length`, is too short for the permutation of size `min`_  `Permutation#apply` was given a list shorter than the permutation's Lehmer code
        ```

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        capture[Permutation.Error](Permutation(Sequence(3, 1, 4, 6, 0, 5)))
        // Permutation.Error(InvalidIndex(6, 5))
        ```
    entry .Reason   why a permutation could not be made or applied
      detail
          An enumeration of `BaseRange`, `DuplicateIndex`, `InvalidIndex` and `TooShort`, each
          with the values the message names; see `Permutation.Error` for when each is raised.
          Each case has a `#number`, 1 to 4, the error's secondary code.
      entry #number   the reason's secondary error code, 1 to 4
    entry .communicable   renders each `Permutation.Error.Reason` as a message
    entry #reason   why the permutation could not be made or applied
  entry #factoradic   the permutation's number, in the factorial number system
  entry #lehmer   the permutation's Lehmer code: for each position, how many smaller indexes come after it
      `#factoradic` expanded into its digits, most significant first, with no leading zeros, so
      it is as long as the suffix the permutation moves. Each digit counts how many of the
      remaining indexes are smaller than the one chosen at that position.
  entry #expansion   the reordered indexes: the permutation applied to `0` to `n - 1`
      The indexes `.apply` was given, for a permutation whose first element moves: for
      `Permutation(Sequence(3, 1, 4, 2, 0, 5))`, `List(3, 1, 4, 2, 0, 5)`. It is as long as
      `#lehmer`, so it omits any leading indexes the permutation leaves in place.
  entry #apply   rearranges a list, or gives the index one position takes its element from
      `permutation(list)` rearranges a list at least as long as `#lehmer`, raising
      `Permutation.Error` with `TooShort` otherwise; a longer list keeps its excess leading
      elements in place and has the rest rearranged.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val shuffle = Permutation(Sequence(3, 1, 4, 2, 0, 5))
      shuffle(List(t"zero", t"one", t"two", t"three", t"four", t"five"))
      // List(t"three", t"one", t"four", t"two", t"zero", t"five")
      shuffle(3)   // 2
      ```

      `permutation(n)` with an `Int` is the `n`th element of `#expansion`, and `n` itself
      beyond it, since a permutation fixes every point outside its domain. It reads a `List`
      by position, so it is linear in `n`.
  entry #inverse   the permutation which undoes this one
      For every list `xs` long enough, `p.inverse(p(xs)) == xs`. The identity is its own
      inverse.

entry .Factoradic   a non-negative integer in the factorial number system, as a Lehmer code is written
  detail
      In the factorial number system, the digit at position *i* from the right has base *i* + 1
      and place value *i*!, so every non-negative integer has one representation, and the
      digit sequences of a given length enumerate the permutations of that length. A
      `Factoradic` holds the number as a `BigInt` (`#number`); `.apply` builds one from its
      digits, most significant first, and `#expand` gives them back.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Factoradic(List(3, 2, 1, 0)) == Factoradic(23)   // 3·3! + 2·2! + 1·1! + 0·0!
      Factoradic(23).expand                            // List(3, 2, 1, 0)
      ```
  refer metamorphose.Permutation
  entry .apply   a factoradic from its digits, most significant first, each checked below its base
      The last digit has base 1, so must be 0; the one before it base 2, and so on. A digit
      which is not below its base raises `Permutation.Error` with `BaseRange`, so it needs a
      `Tactic[Permutation.Error]`. The case-class constructor `Factoradic(number)` takes the
      `BigInt` directly.
  entry #number   the integer, as a `BigInt`
  entry #expand   the number's digits, most significant first, with no leading zeros
      `Factoradic(0).expand` is `Nil`; otherwise the list ends in the always-zero units digit.
      This is a permutation's `#lehmer`.

entry .Factorial   factorials as `BigInt`s, and the factorial sequences the factoradic encoding uses
  detail
      An object of utilities over factorials, computed exactly with `BigInt` arithmetic.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Factorial(4)   // 24
      Factorial(21)  // 51090942171709440000
      ```
  entry .apply   `n!`, as a `BigInt`
      `Factorial(0)` is 1. The argument should be non-negative.
  entry .magnitude   one more than the number of factoradic digits needed to write `n`
      The smallest `i` for which `(i - 1)!` exceeds `n`: the size of the place-value table
      `Factoradic#expand` needs, plus one.
  entry .sequence   the factorials from `(n - 1)!` down to `0!`, as a list of `n` `BigInt`s
      The place values of an `n`-digit factoradic number, most significant first:
      `Factorial.sequence(3)` is `List(2, 1, 1)`.
